8
00:00:18.137 --> 00:00:22.191
What you get: the rounds that decide levels as often as design does.

9
00:00:22.471 --> 00:00:31.047
It covers: what behavioural and leadership interviews assess at each level, and the story format;. how to build a story bank mapped to competencies;.

10
00:00:31.327 --> 00:00:38.345
I versus we, and how to back impact claims with honest evidence;. the question families and what each one probes;.

11
00:00:39.695 --> 00:00:48.591
technical leadership as a senior IC, and principal-level strategy and org influence;. planning which story goes to which interviewer across a loop.

12
00:00:48.871 --> 00:00:52.135
What the labs measured (on an illustrative 8-story bank):

13
00:00:52.415 --> 00:00:59.178
L1 (coverage): for a staff loop, 1 gap (prioritisation) and 10 thin or below-level competencies.

14
00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:01.933
Three stories carry four competencies each.

15
00:01:02.213 --> 00:01:12.197
L2 (lint): the two weak stories fail on everything: under 30 seconds, set-up above 35%, we in place of I, no numbers, one-line learnings.

16
00:01:12.477 --> 00:01:13.629
The rewrites pass.

17
00:01:14.979 --> 00:01:25.176
L3 (evidence, real PostgreSQL): a covering index cut p50 by 38%, 37% and 11% in three rounds.

18
00:01:25.456 --> 00:01:33.264
Each round's confidence interval was tight, but the rounds disagreed with each other, and p99 showed no measurable change.

19
00:01:33.544 --> 00:01:37.085
Cherry-picking the same data gives 99% faster.

20
00:01:37.365 --> 00:01:45.536
L4 (loop plan): after the gap is filled, all 12 questions have a fitting story (fit 19, then 22 of 24).

21
00:01:45.816 --> 00:01:50.808
Even the optimal plan tells 3 stories twice, which means more stories are needed.

