00:01:56.049 --> 00:01:57.969
Introduction and Why It Matters.

26
00:01:59.219 --> 00:02:00.968
A story: we did great work.

27
00:02:01.248 --> 00:02:04.768
Rahul was a strong engineer with 15 years of experience.

28
00:02:05.048 --> 00:02:10.829
His design round was rated staff level, but his behavioural round came back senior, not staff.

29
00:02:11.109 --> 00:02:12.069
The feedback:

30
00:02:12.349 --> 00:02:13.651
Every answer was 'we'.

31
00:02:13.931 --> 00:02:15.893
I couldn't tell what he personally did.

32
00:02:16.173 --> 00:02:17.709
No numbers on impact.

33
00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:21.125
His conflict example was with a junior engineer.

34
00:02:21.405 --> 00:02:23.368
We needed influence across teams.

35
00:02:23.648 --> 00:02:27.083
When asked what he'd do differently, he said 'nothing, it went well'.

36
00:02:28.433 --> 00:02:30.075
He had done staff-level work.

37
00:02:30.355 --> 00:02:31.507
He hadn't shown it.

38
00:02:31.787 --> 00:02:36.609
Behavioural rounds are evidence interviews: the interviewer can only score what you say.

39
00:02:36.889 --> 00:02:42.734
Stories need to be concrete, in the first person, quantified and reflective, and matched to the level.

40
00:02:44.084 --> 00:02:44.767
Quote:

41
00:02:45.047 --> 00:02:46.711
The thesis of this chapter.

42
00:02:46.991 --> 00:02:52.260
Behavioural rounds score evidence of scope, judgement and influence at your target level.

43
00:02:52.540 --> 00:02:56.593
Build a story bank that covers the competencies at that level (L1).

44
00:02:56.873 --> 00:03:01.161
Tell each story in first person with numbers and a real learning (L2).

45
00:03:01.441 --> 00:03:04.663
Back impact claims with measurements you can defend (L3).

46
00:03:04.943 --> 00:03:08.527
Plan the loop so each interviewer hears different evidence (L4).

