154
00:07:02.747 --> 00:07:03.941
The story format:

155
00:07:04.221 --> 00:07:05.309
STAR + L.

156
00:07:05.589 --> 00:07:06.933
Here is a quick table:

157
00:07:07.213 --> 00:07:08.899
Part, and Share, and Content.

158
00:07:09.179 --> 00:07:10.267
Situation.

159
00:07:10.547 --> 00:07:12.317
Share: ~15%.

160
00:07:12.597 --> 00:07:13.429
Content:

161
00:07:13.709 --> 00:07:15.800
Context, with one number for scale.

162
00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:16.805
Task.

163
00:07:17.085 --> 00:07:18.707
Share: ~10%.

164
00:07:18.987 --> 00:07:19.819
Content:

165
00:07:20.099 --> 00:07:22.061
Your responsibility and the stakes.

166
00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:23.131
Action.

167
00:07:23.411 --> 00:07:25.075
Share: ~50%.

168
00:07:25.355 --> 00:07:26.187
Content:

169
00:07:26.467 --> 00:07:29.347
What you did, in order, with your reasoning and trade-offs.

170
00:07:29.627 --> 00:07:30.459
Result.

171
00:07:30.739 --> 00:07:32.509
Share: ~15%.

172
00:07:32.789 --> 00:07:33.621
Content:

173
00:07:33.901 --> 00:07:36.312
Numbers, what changed, what outlasted you.

174
00:07:36.592 --> 00:07:37.360
Learning.

175
00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:39.261
Share: ~10%.

176
00:07:39.541 --> 00:07:40.373
Content:

177
00:07:40.653 --> 00:07:42.296
What you do differently since then.

178
00:07:43.646 --> 00:07:44.691
Spoken length:

179
00:07:44.971 --> 00:07:49.622
90 to 150 seconds for the first telling, then let the follow-ups come.

180
00:07:49.902 --> 00:07:58.115
L2 flags set-up over 35%, we outnumbering I in the action, results without numbers, and learnings under 10 words.

181
00:07:59.465 --> 00:08:00.553
The story bank.

182
00:08:00.833 --> 00:08:02.561
Aim for 8 to 12 stories.

183
00:08:02.841 --> 00:08:08.580
Each covers 2 to 4 competencies at the target level, from the last 3 to 5 years where possible.

184
00:08:08.860 --> 00:08:14.343
Tag each story with its competencies and level, and keep the notes in the STAR+L format.

185
00:08:14.623 --> 00:08:17.780
The bank in §5 is an illustrative example.

186
00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:22.455
Check coverage against the competencies expected at your target level (L1).

187
00:08:22.735 --> 00:08:26.873
Include required types: a real failure with real consequences;.

188
00:08:28.223 --> 00:08:38.975
a disagreement with someone more senior;. an incident;. mentoring;. a cross-team delivery;. a decision not to do something;. for B4, a strategy or org change.

189
00:08:39.255 --> 00:08:41.047
Avoid single points of failure.

190
00:08:41.327 --> 00:08:46.981
A story tagged for 4 plus competencies gets reused across interviewers (L4).

191
00:08:48.331 --> 00:08:50.144
I, we and specificity.

192
00:08:50.424 --> 00:08:53.005
Use we for context and I for actions.

193
00:08:53.285 --> 00:08:55.163
The team decided hides your role.

194
00:08:55.443 --> 00:09:00.413
I proposed X, wrote the comparison, and ran the review that adopted it shows it.

195
00:09:00.693 --> 00:09:02.741
Name decisions and their reasoning.

196
00:09:03.021 --> 00:09:06.051
I chose to roll back rather than fix forward because...

197
00:09:06.331 --> 00:09:09.168
Be specific: dates, numbers, names of systems.

198
00:09:09.448 --> 00:09:11.453
Use colleagues' roles rather than names.

199
00:09:11.733 --> 00:09:13.163
Own mistakes directly.

200
00:09:13.443 --> 00:09:17.517
I only sampled English queries, and I signed off (S3 rewrite).

201
00:09:17.797 --> 00:09:19.376
Not The testing missed Hindi.

202
00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:22.091
Evidence for impact.

203
00:09:22.371 --> 00:09:24.291
Senior interviewers probe numbers:

204
00:09:24.571 --> 00:09:25.830
How did you measure that?

205
00:09:26.110 --> 00:09:30.227
L3 shows three honest-evidence problems in a single experiment:

206
00:09:30.507 --> 00:09:31.745
Rounds disagree.

207
00:09:32.025 --> 00:09:43.502
Each round's 95% confidence interval was tight, yet the p50 reduction ranged from 11% to 38% across rounds as caching conditions changed.

208
00:09:43.782 --> 00:09:46.491
A single benchmark understates uncertainty.

209
00:09:46.771 --> 00:09:49.587
Production metrics over weeks are better evidence.

210
00:09:49.867 --> 00:09:56.395
Percentiles differ. p50 improved in every round, but p99 had no measurable change.

211
00:09:56.675 --> 00:09:58.233
Faster needs a percentile.

212
00:09:59.583 --> 00:10:00.927
Cherry-picking inflates.

213
00:10:01.207 --> 00:10:05.836
The worst before run against the best after run said 99% faster.

214
00:10:06.116 --> 00:10:08.633
A single pair of queries said 70%.

215
00:10:08.913 --> 00:10:09.596
Say:

216
00:10:09.876 --> 00:10:19.711
Median page-load for order history dropped by roughly a third in our load tests, and in production the p50 fell from X to Y over the following month.

217
00:10:19.991 --> 00:10:22.935
The tail didn't move; that came from a separate change.

218
00:10:23.215 --> 00:10:26.009
If you don't know the number, say how you would have measured it.

219
00:10:27.359 --> 00:10:29.535
Question families and what they probe.

