Standards.

326
00:13:39.356 --> 00:13:40.465
Strong elements:

327
00:13:40.745 --> 00:13:42.836
Rubrics, calibration, outcomes.

328
00:13:43.116 --> 00:13:43.820
Bank:

329
00:13:44.100 --> 00:13:45.039
S7.

330
00:13:46.389 --> 00:13:48.223
A full answer, with follow-ups.

331
00:13:48.503 --> 00:13:49.399
Interviewer:

332
00:13:49.679 --> 00:13:54.437
Tell me about a time you disagreed with someone more senior on a technical decision.

333
00:13:54.717 --> 00:13:57.341
Candidate (S2, ~100 s):

334
00:13:57.621 --> 00:14:04.959
Two years ago our principal architect proposed event sourcing for the whole order domain, about 30 services' worth of state.

335
00:14:05.239 --> 00:14:11.127
I thought it would slow the team for a year with little benefit, because only payments needed a full audit trail.

336
00:14:11.407 --> 00:14:16.143
I owned the order-history and payments APIs, so I'd be carrying a lot of the cost.

337
00:14:16.423 --> 00:14:25.618
I wrote a two-page comparison of three options: full event sourcing, a hybrid with a ledger for payments and an outbox elsewhere, and the status quo.

338
00:14:25.898 --> 00:14:29.759
I included a prototype of the order-history query both ways.

339
00:14:30.039 --> 00:14:39.277
I met him one-to-one before the review so he wouldn't be surprised, and I told him what would change my mind: if we needed temporal queries across orders within a year.

340
00:14:40.627 --> 00:14:42.397
The review adopted the hybrid.

341
00:14:42.677 --> 00:14:51.019
The ledger shipped in six weeks, against five months estimated for the full approach, and two years later we still hadn't needed full event sourcing.

342
00:14:51.299 --> 00:15:00.280
What I learned is that a prototype, a pre-meeting and a stated condition for changing my mind make disagreement land as help, not opposition.

343
00:15:00.560 --> 00:15:01.456
Interviewer:

344
00:15:01.736 --> 00:15:03.421
How did he react in the one-to-one?

345
00:15:03.701 --> 00:15:04.619
Candidate:

346
00:15:04.899 --> 00:15:07.288
He pushed back on the audit argument at first.

347
00:15:07.568 --> 00:15:11.877
Then he pointed out that we'd need replay for refunds, which I hadn't considered.

348
00:15:12.157 --> 00:15:14.120
That became part of the ledger design.

349
00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:18.069
So the hybrid was partly his idea by the time of the review.

350
00:15:18.349 --> 00:15:19.245
Interviewer:

351
00:15:19.525 --> 00:15:21.936
What if the review had gone with full event sourcing?

352
00:15:23.286 --> 00:15:24.203
Candidate:

353
00:15:24.483 --> 00:15:25.785
I'd have committed to it.

354
00:15:26.065 --> 00:15:33.574
I'd have asked for the revisit metric to be recorded in the ADR, and focused my team on making the order-history projection cheap.

355
00:15:33.854 --> 00:15:34.750
Interviewer:

356
00:15:35.030 --> 00:15:36.374
What would you do differently?

357
00:15:36.654 --> 00:15:37.571
Candidate:

358
00:15:37.851 --> 00:15:40.027
I'd involve the product manager earlier.

359
00:15:40.307 --> 00:15:44.617
The delivery date was the strongest argument, and it would have come better from them.

360
00:15:44.897 --> 00:15:46.411
Why it scores at B3:

361
00:15:46.691 --> 00:15:49.102
The disagreement is with a senior person.

362
00:15:49.382 --> 00:15:56.251
The candidate has evidence (a prototype), a stated condition for changing their mind, and a quantified outcome.

363
00:15:57.601 --> 00:15:59.777
They incorporated the other person's point.

364
00:16:00.057 --> 00:16:03.492
They show disagree-and-commit readiness and a real differently.

365
00:16:04.842 --> 00:16:07.231
Technical leadership as a senior IC.

366
00:16:07.511 --> 00:16:11.949
Leadership questions for staff engineers probe mechanisms, not charisma:

367
00:16:13.299 --> 00:16:14.643
Here is a quick table:

368
00:16:14.923 --> 00:16:17.547
Mechanism, and What good looks like, and Chapter.

369
00:16:17.827 --> 00:16:18.957
Design reviews.

370
00:16:19.237 --> 00:16:20.453
What good looks like:

371
00:16:20.733 --> 00:16:24.019
Clear criteria, written feedback, decisions recorded.

372
00:16:24.299 --> 00:16:25.152
Chapter:

373
00:16:25.432 --> 00:16:26.627
Ch 32.

374
00:16:26.907 --> 00:16:28.272
ADRs and RFCs.

375
00:16:28.552 --> 00:16:29.768
What good looks like:

376
00:16:30.048 --> 00:16:32.352
Options, trade-offs, revisit triggers.

377
00:16:32.632 --> 00:16:33.485
Chapter:

378
00:16:33.765 --> 00:16:34.981
Ch 31.

379
00:16:35.261 --> 00:16:36.947
Standards and paved roads.

380
00:16:37.227 --> 00:16:38.443
What good looks like:

381
00:16:38.723 --> 00:16:41.432
Adopted because they're easier, not mandated.

382
00:16:41.712 --> 00:16:42.565
Chapter:

383
00:16:42.845 --> 00:16:44.147
Ch 37.

384
00:16:44.427 --> 00:16:45.429
Migrations.

385
00:16:45.709 --> 00:16:46.925
What good looks like:

386
00:16:47.205 --> 00:16:51.259
Visible cost, libraries, milestones, escalation with fixes.

387
00:16:51.539 --> 00:16:52.392
Chapter:

388
00:16:52.672 --> 00:16:53.589
S6.

389
00:16:53.869 --> 00:16:55.064
Incident culture.

390
00:16:55.344 --> 00:16:56.560
What good looks like:

391
00:16:56.840 --> 00:16:59.741
Blameless postmortems, owned actions, followed through.

392
