Chapter:

393
00:17:01.155 --> 00:17:03.096
S1, Ch 27.

394
00:17:03.376 --> 00:17:04.997
Mentoring and sponsorship.

395
00:17:05.277 --> 00:17:06.493
What good looks like:

396
00:17:06.773 --> 00:17:09.547
Growing seniors, giving visible work to others.

397
00:17:09.827 --> 00:17:10.680
Chapter:

398
00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:11.835
S4.

399
00:17:12.115 --> 00:17:13.096
Managing up.

400
00:17:13.376 --> 00:17:14.592
What good looks like:

401
00:17:14.872 --> 00:17:18.264
Risks early, options not problems, decisions with dates.

402
00:17:18.544 --> 00:17:19.397
Chapter:

403
00:17:19.677 --> 00:17:20.616
S9.

404
00:17:21.966 --> 00:17:23.267
Disagree and commit.

405
00:17:23.547 --> 00:17:24.763
What good looks like:

406
00:17:25.043 --> 00:17:27.475
Argue with evidence, then execute fully.

407
00:17:27.755 --> 00:17:28.609
Chapter:

408
00:17:28.889 --> 00:17:29.742
S2.

409
00:17:31.092 --> 00:17:32.543
Principal-level signals.

410
00:17:32.823 --> 00:17:34.913
At B4, interviewers look for:

411
00:17:35.193 --> 00:17:36.409
Choosing problems.

412
00:17:36.689 --> 00:17:41.980
I noticed that 40% of our roadmap slipped each quarter, and made that the problem (S9).

413
00:17:42.260 --> 00:17:44.628
This is more than solving the problems you're given.

414
00:17:44.908 --> 00:17:47.041
Multi-year bets with reversibility.

415
00:17:47.321 --> 00:17:49.284
Not yet, with a trigger (S5).

416
00:17:49.564 --> 00:17:50.823
Knowing when to stop.

417
00:17:51.103 --> 00:17:52.660
Changing systems of work.

418
00:17:52.940 --> 00:17:59.831
Planning processes, hiring bars (S7), architecture review, paved roads, and not just shipping systems.

419
00:18:01.181 --> 00:18:02.887
Influence upward and outward.

420
00:18:03.167 --> 00:18:09.077
Directors, executives, other orgs, and sometimes the industry: talks, open source, standards.

421
00:18:09.357 --> 00:18:11.127
Stories told at the org level.

422
00:18:11.407 --> 00:18:13.477
Cost, risk and people outcomes.

423
00:18:13.757 --> 00:18:14.973
Failure at scale.

424
00:18:15.253 --> 00:18:19.903
A bet that didn't work, how you noticed, and what you changed in how the org decides.

425
00:18:21.253 --> 00:18:23.387
Follow-up probes and how to prepare.

426
00:18:23.667 --> 00:18:25.011
Here is a quick table:

427
00:18:25.291 --> 00:18:27.360
Probe, and Why they ask, and Prepare.

428
00:18:27.640 --> 00:18:29.112
What exactly did you do?

429
00:18:29.392 --> 00:18:30.416
Why they ask:

430
00:18:30.696 --> 00:18:32.403
Separate your role from the team's.

431
00:18:32.683 --> 00:18:33.493
Prepare:

432
00:18:33.773 --> 00:18:35.416
Actions in the first person.

433
00:18:35.696 --> 00:18:36.955
How did you measure that?

434
00:18:37.235 --> 00:18:38.259
Why they ask:

435
00:18:38.539 --> 00:18:39.669
Test the result.

436
00:18:39.949 --> 00:18:40.760
Prepare:

437
00:18:41.040 --> 00:18:42.576
L3-style evidence.

438
00:18:42.856 --> 00:18:44.200
What would you do differently?

439
00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:45.504
Why they ask:

440
00:18:45.784 --> 00:18:46.851
Self-awareness.

441
00:18:47.131 --> 00:18:47.941
Prepare:

442
00:18:48.221 --> 00:18:49.843
A real, specific change.

443
00:18:50.123 --> 00:18:52.597
What did your manager or the other person think?

444
00:18:52.877 --> 00:18:53.901
Why they ask:

445
00:18:54.181 --> 00:18:55.440
Perspective-taking.

446
00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:56.531
Prepare:

447
00:18:56.811 --> 00:18:57.920
Their view, fairly.

448
00:18:58.200 --> 00:18:59.373
What happened next?

449
00:18:59.653 --> 00:19:00.677
Why they ask:

450
00:19:00.957 --> 00:19:01.917
Durability.

451
00:19:02.197 --> 00:19:03.008
Prepare:

452
00:19:03.288 --> 00:19:04.483
What outlasted you.

453
00:19:04.763 --> 00:19:06.171
What was the hardest part?

454
00:19:06.451 --> 00:19:07.475
Why they ask:

455
00:19:07.755 --> 00:19:08.437
Depth.

456
00:19:08.717 --> 00:19:09.528
Prepare:

457
00:19:09.808 --> 00:19:10.981
The judgement call.

458
00:19:11.261 --> 00:19:12.648
Did anyone disagree?

459
00:19:12.928 --> 00:19:13.952
Why they ask:

460
00:19:14.232 --> 00:19:15.661
Politics and influence.

461
00:19:15.941 --> 00:19:16.752
Prepare:

462
00:19:17.032 --> 00:19:18.205
How you handled it.

463
00:19:19.555 --> 00:19:20.857
Difficult questions.

464
00:19:21.137 --> 00:19:22.246
Why are you leaving?

465
00:19:22.526 --> 00:19:24.318
Talk about what you're moving towards.

466
00:19:24.598 --> 00:19:26.454
Don't criticise your current employer.

467
00:19:26.734 --> 00:19:28.185
What's your biggest weakness?

468
00:19:28.465 --> 00:19:30.577
A real one, with what you do about it.

469
00:19:30.857 --> 00:19:32.265
Not a disguised strength.

470
00:19:32.545 --> 00:19:34.614
Tell me about a gap or a short tenure.

471
00:19:34.894 --> 00:19:38.542
Brief, honest and factual, then move to what you did or learned.

472
00:19:38.822 --> 00:19:40.507
Tell me about a time you were wrong.

473
00:19:40.787 --> 00:19:42.515
Treat it like a failure story.

474
00:19:42.795 --> 00:19:45.291
Change your mind visibly and with evidence.

475
00:19:46.641 --> 00:19:47.793
Why this company?

476
00:19:48.073 --> 00:19:53.215
Specific to their product and their technical challenges, connected to your experience.

477
00:19:53.495 --> 00:19:55.436
Where do you see yourself in 5 years?

478
00:19:55.716 --> 00:20:00.004
A direction (for example deeper technical leadership), not a job title.

479
00:20:01.354 --> 00:20:02.399
Planning the loop.

480
00:20:02.679 --> 00:20:07.117
Interviewers in a loop often cover different competencies, and they compare notes.

481
00:20:07.397 --> 00:20:11.045
Telling the same story three times makes the evidence look thin.

482
00:20:11.325 --> 00:20:15.805
Before the loop, ask the recruiter which competencies each interview focuses on.

483
00:20:16.085 --> 00:20:18.773
This is often shared (VERIFY per company).

484
00:20:19.053 --> 00:20:22.957
Plan a primary and a backup story per likely question (L4).

485
00:20:23.237 --> 00:20:28.186
Keep each story to at most two interviewers, and never repeat one with the same interviewer.

486
00:20:29.536 --> 00:20:30.624
Stay flexible.

487
00:20:30.904 --> 00:20:35.235
If an interviewer asks something unplanned, pick the best-fitting unused story.

488
00:20:36.585 --> 00:20:38.505
Values and leadership principles.

489
00:20:38.785 --> 00:20:44.118
Some companies publish values or leadership principles, and their interviewers score against them.

490
00:20:44.398 --> 00:20:49.347
Read the published list and map your stories to it, using the company's wording in your tags.

491
00:20:49.627 --> 00:20:51.185
Don't recite the principles.

492
00:20:51.465 --> 00:20:52.659
Show the behaviour.

493
00:20:52.939 --> 00:20:58.038
Lists change, and their interpretation varies between teams (VERIFY the current version).

494
00:20:59.388 --> 00:21:00.284
