565
00:24:49.712 --> 00:24:57.200
S1 Checkout outage in the festival sale. - competencies: incident, pressure, ownership, communication. - level:

566
00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:59.165
B3. - situation:

567
00:24:59.445 --> 00:25:08.171
During the festival sale our checkout error rate went from 0.2% to 18% within four minutes of the 8 pm deal drop.

568
00:25:08.451 --> 00:25:11.544
I was the on-call lead for the checkout domain that night.

569
00:25:12.894 --> 00:25:13.641
- task:

570
00:25:13.921 --> 00:25:22.411
I had to restore checkout quickly, keep leadership and customer support informed, and make sure it could not happen the same way again. - action:

571
00:25:22.691 --> 00:25:30.414
I declared a SEV1, split the bridge into a mitigation lead and a communications lead, and asked for the last three changes.

572
00:25:30.694 --> 00:25:36.262
I found that a pricing cache TTL change had moved load onto the promotions database.

573
00:25:36.542 --> 00:25:43.646
I rolled back the config, turned on the static-price fallback for the deal SKUs, and posted updates every ten minutes.

574
00:25:43.926 --> 00:25:54.806
Afterwards I ran the blameless postmortem and owned three of the actions: a load test for sale-day config, a canary rule for TTL changes, and a runbook for the fallback.

575
00:25:56.156 --> 00:25:56.967
- result:

576
00:25:57.247 --> 00:25:59.423
Checkout recovered in 23 minutes.

577
00:25:59.703 --> 00:26:02.945
We estimated about 41,000 affected sessions.

578
00:26:03.225 --> 00:26:09.924
The next two sales ran without a checkout SEV, and TTL changes now go through a canary. - learning:

579
00:26:10.204 --> 00:26:17.649
I now ask for what changed in the first five minutes of every incident, and I push for config changes to be treated like code.

580
00:26:17.929 --> 00:26:26.420
S2 Disagreeing with the principal architect on event sourcing. - competencies: conflict, technical-decision, influence. - level:

581
00:26:26.700 --> 00:26:27.575
B3.

582
00:26:28.925 --> 00:26:29.949
- situation:

583
00:26:30.229 --> 00:26:34.325
The principal architect proposed event sourcing for the whole order domain.

584
00:26:34.605 --> 00:26:38.679
I believed it would slow the team for a year with little benefit. - task:

585
00:26:38.959 --> 00:26:44.485
I needed to change the decision without damaging the relationship, and stay open to being wrong.

586
00:26:45.835 --> 00:26:46.624
- action:

587
00:26:46.904 --> 00:26:54.243
I wrote a two-page comparison with three options and costs, including a prototype of the order history query both ways.

588
00:26:54.523 --> 00:27:04.677
I met him one-to-one before the review so he wasn't surprised, and I proposed event sourcing only for the payment ledger, where the audit trail mattered, with an outbox for the rest.

589
00:27:04.957 --> 00:27:08.136
I agreed a metric that would make us revisit it. - result:

590
00:27:08.416 --> 00:27:10.187
The review adopted the hybrid.

591
00:27:10.467 --> 00:27:19.896
The ledger shipped in six weeks instead of the estimated five months for the full approach, and two years later we still had not needed full event sourcing for orders.

592
00:27:21.246 --> 00:27:22.035
- learning:

593
00:27:22.315 --> 00:27:28.118
Disagreement lands better with a prototype, a pre-meeting and a condition for changing my mind.

594
00:27:28.398 --> 00:27:34.115
S3 My failed search migration. - competencies: failure, ownership, learning. - level:

595
00:27:34.395 --> 00:27:35.974
B2. - situation:

596
00:27:36.254 --> 00:27:42.697
We were moving product search from a self-hosted cluster to a new version and I planned the cutover. - task:

597
00:27:42.977 --> 00:27:44.982
Cut over with no visible impact.

598
00:27:46.332 --> 00:27:47.121
- action:

599
00:27:47.401 --> 00:27:52.031
We did the cutover on a Tuesday and we saw that relevance was worse for Hindi queries.

600
00:27:52.311 --> 00:27:54.081
We rolled back after two hours.

601
00:27:54.361 --> 00:27:58.500
We had tested only English queries in the shadow comparison. - result:

602
00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:00.529
It was rolled back. - learning:

603
00:28:00.809 --> 00:28:02.132
Test more languages.

604
00:28:02.412 --> 00:28:09.921
S4 Mentoring a new engineer into on-call. - competencies: mentoring, growing-others, communication. - level:

605
00:28:10.201 --> 00:28:11.076
B2.

606
00:28:12.426 --> 00:28:13.450
- situation:

607
00:28:13.730 --> 00:28:20.642
A new engineer, Priya, joined the dispatch team and was anxious about joining on-call after four weeks. - task:

608
00:28:20.922 --> 00:28:25.018
Get her confident and effective on-call without throwing her in. - action:

609
00:28:25.298 --> 00:28:30.482
I paired with her on three shadow shifts, then reverse-shadowed, where she led and I watched.

610
00:28:30.762 --> 00:28:38.933
I gave her ownership of rewriting two runbooks she had found confusing, and we did a weekly 30-minute review of real alerts. - result:

611
00:28:39.213 --> 00:28:41.218
She joined the rotation in week seven.

612
00:28:41.498 --> 00:28:50.138
Her runbook rewrites cut the median time to mitigate for those two alerts from 22 to 9 minutes, and she later mentored the next new joiner the same way.

613
00:28:51.488 --> 00:28:52.277
- learning:

614
00:28:52.557 --> 00:28:56.675
Ownership of something small builds confidence faster than more shadowing.

615
00:28:56.955 --> 00:29:04.869
S5 Choosing not to build a feature store. - competencies: technical-decision, strategy, cost, ambiguity. - level:

616
00:29:05.149 --> 00:29:06.771
B4. - situation:

617
00:29:07.051 --> 00:29:11.808
Three teams asked for a feature store for recommendations, fraud and ETA models.

618
00:29:12.088 --> 00:29:15.843
The vendor quotes were large and the requirements were vague. - task:

619
00:29:16.123 --> 00:29:19.941
I was asked to recommend a platform direction for the next 18 months.

620
00:29:21.291 --> 00:29:22.081
- action:

621
00:29:22.361 --> 00:29:26.393
I interviewed the three teams and measured how features were actually used:

622
00:29:26.673 --> 00:29:32.539
80% were batch features read once a day, and only ETA needed sub-second online features.

623
00:29:32.819 --> 00:29:45.790
I proposed a shared offline feature table with conventions, plus a small online store only for ETA, and wrote an ADR with a trigger to revisit if two more teams needed online features. - result:

624
00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:57.441
We saved roughly the vendor cost for the year, the ETA team shipped in one quarter, and the revisit trigger fired 14 months later, at which point we bought a managed store with clear requirements.

625
00:29:58.791 --> 00:29:59.580
- learning:

626
00:29:59.860 --> 00:30:05.172
A clear not yet with a trigger is often better than a big platform bet on vague requirements.

627
00:30:05.452 --> 00:30:14.412
S6 Aligning four teams on an API deprecation. - competencies: influence, cross-team, delivery, communication. - level:

628
00:30:14.692 --> 00:30:16.377
B3. - situation:

629
00:30:16.657 --> 00:30:24.551
We had to retire the v1 cart API, which four teams still called, before a payment-provider change deadline. - task:

630
00:30:24.831 --> 00:30:28.713
I had no authority over the other teams but owned the deadline risk.

631
00:30:30.063 --> 00:30:30.853
- action:

632
00:30:31.133 --> 00:30:46.002
I published the call volumes per team, offered a migration library and office hours, set dated milestones with each team lead, and escalated one team's blocker early to their director with a proposed fix rather than a complaint. - result:

633
00:30:46.282 --> 00:30:53.599
All four teams migrated nine days before the deadline and v1 traffic went to zero without an incident. - learning:

634
00:30:53.879 --> 00:30:57.485
Making the cost visible and the path easy did more than asking.

635
00:30:57.765 --> 00:31:00.282
S7 Hiring bar for the platform team.

636
00:31:01.632 --> 00:31:05.216
- competencies: hiring, growing-others, strategy. - level:

637
00:31:05.496 --> 00:31:07.117
B4. - situation:

638
00:31:07.397 --> 00:31:14.757
Our platform team's hiring had high variance: two recent hires struggled and interviewers disagreed often. - task:

639
00:31:15.037 --> 00:31:20.712
As the senior engineer on the hiring panel, improve our hiring decisions without slowing hiring.

640
00:31:22.062 --> 00:31:22.851
- action:

641
00:31:23.131 --> 00:31:35.505
I introduced anchored rubrics per interview, had interviewers write feedback before the debrief, ran four calibration sessions on recorded interviews, and tracked each interviewer's pass rate monthly. - result:

642
00:31:35.785 --> 00:31:46.793
Verdict agreement in shadow scoring rose from about two-thirds to over eighty percent, time to hire stayed the same, and the next six hires all passed their six-month review. - learning:

643
00:31:47.073 --> 00:31:52.598
Structure beats instinct in hiring, and interviewers welcome it when it saves them debate time.

644
00:31:52.878 --> 00:31:55.694
S8 Customer-facing delay in slot booking.

645
00:31:57.044 --> 00:32:01.695
- competencies: customer-focus, ambiguity, technical-decision. - level:

646
00:32:01.975 --> 00:32:03.553
B2. - situation:

647
00:32:03.833 --> 00:32:09.615
Customers complained that the slot they booked showed as full when they returned to change the order. - task:

648
00:32:09.895 --> 00:32:13.820
I was asked to fix the complaints in the slot booking service. - action:

649
00:32:14.100 --> 00:32:17.449
We found the capacity check counted the customer's own booking.

650
00:32:17.729 --> 00:32:23.276
We changed the query to exclude the customer's own reservation and added a test. - result:

651
00:32:23.556 --> 00:32:24.815
Complaints went down.

652
00:32:26.165 --> 00:32:26.954
- learning:

653
00:32:27.234 --> 00:32:30.050
Look at the customer journey, not just the API.

654
00:32:31.400 --> 00:32:35.219
Example: storiesv2.md, rewrites and one new story.

655
00:32:35.499 --> 00:32:41.003
Version 2: rewrites of the two weakest stories, plus a new story for the gap L1 found.

656
00:32:41.283 --> 00:32:46.000
S3 My failed search migration. - competencies: failure, ownership, learning. - level:

657
00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:48.965
B3. - situation:

658
00:32:49.245 --> 00:32:52.659
I led the cutover of product search to a new engine version.

659
00:32:52.939 --> 00:32:56.416
About 30% of our queries are in Hindi or Hinglish.

660
00:32:57.766 --> 00:32:58.513
- task:

661
00:32:58.793 --> 00:33:04.894
I owned the plan and the go or no-go decision, with a goal of no visible relevance drop. - action:

662
00:33:05.174 --> 00:33:10.465
I built the shadow comparison, but I only sampled English queries, and I signed off on it.

663
00:33:10.745 --> 00:33:14.670
Two hours after the cutover, support flagged bad Hindi results.

664
00:33:14.950 --> 00:33:21.542
I made the call to roll back, then led the analysis: the new analyzer tokenised Devanagari differently.

665
00:33:21.822 --> 00:33:31.443
I added a language-stratified query sample and a click-through guardrail to the shadow test, fixed the analyzer config, and re-ran the cutover three weeks later.

666
00:33:32.793 --> 00:33:33.604
- result:

667
00:33:33.884 --> 00:33:40.583
The second cutover held Hindi click-through within 1% of the old engine and improved English by 4%.

668
00:33:40.863 --> 00:33:45.684
The stratified shadow test is now the template for every search change. - learning:

669
00:33:45.964 --> 00:33:55.351
I now ask which users does this sample not represent? before signing off any comparison, and I say out loud which go or no-go criteria are mine.

670
00:33:55.631 --> 00:34:02.927
S8 Customer-facing delay in slot booking. - competencies: customer-focus, ambiguity, technical-decision.

671
00:34:04.277 --> 00:34:05.023
- level:

672
00:34:05.303 --> 00:34:06.882
B2. - situation:

673
00:34:07.162 --> 00:34:11.557
Customers who returned to change a delivery slot saw their own slot as full.

674
00:34:11.837 --> 00:34:15.890
Support tickets about it had grown to about 900 a week. - task:

675
00:34:16.170 --> 00:34:22.442
I owned the slot booking service and took the ticket theme on even though the root cause was unclear. - action:

676
00:34:22.722 --> 00:34:31.725
I reproduced the journey end to end rather than testing the API alone, and found that the capacity check counted the customer's own reservation.

677
00:34:32.005 --> 00:34:40.133
I changed the query to exclude it, added a test for the modify flow, and asked support to tag slot tickets so we could see the trend.

678
00:34:41.483 --> 00:34:42.293
- result:

679
00:34:42.573 --> 00:34:51.747
Slot-related tickets fell from about 900 to under 60 a week within two weeks, and modify-order completion rose by 7%. - learning:

680
00:34:52.027 --> 00:35:00.453
I test changes as a customer journey now, not only as API calls, and I ask support for tagged tickets before and after a fix.

681
00:35:00.733 --> 00:35:08.712
S9 Cutting the quarter's roadmap in half. - competencies: prioritisation, strategy, conflict, org-change. - level:

682
00:35:08.992 --> 00:35:09.867
B4.

683
00:35:11.217 --> 00:35:12.241
- situation:

684
00:35:12.521 --> 00:35:22.569
Our platform group of five teams had 23 committed roadmap items for the quarter and delivered about 40% of commitments in the previous two quarters. - task:

685
00:35:22.849 --> 00:35:29.739
As the group's senior architect, I was asked by the director to make the plan credible without losing the business's trust.

686
00:35:31.089 --> 00:35:31.879
- action:

687
00:35:32.159 --> 00:35:44.639
I scored every item on customer impact, risk reduction and cost of delay with each product manager, published the scores openly, and proposed committing to 11 items with the rest as stretch.

688
00:35:44.919 --> 00:35:51.041
I met the two stakeholders whose items dropped before the review, offering them dates and interim workarounds.

689
00:35:51.321 --> 00:35:59.940
I also changed our planning so that each team reserves 20% for operational work, which had been silently eating the roadmap. - result:

690
00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:05.553
We delivered 10 of the 11 commitments that quarter and 85% the quarter after.

691
00:36:05.833 --> 00:36:09.737
Escalations about missed dates dropped from about six a month to one.

692
00:36:11.087 --> 00:36:11.877
- learning:

693
00:36:12.157 --> 00:36:18.087
Saying no with published criteria and a date for later keeps trust; hidden overcommitment loses it.

694
00:36:19.437 --> 00:36:21.784
L1, Does the story bank cover the level?

695
00:36:22.064 --> 00:36:23.003
Requirement:

696
00:36:23.283 --> 00:36:29.597
Every competency expected at my target level has a story, ideally two, at least one at that level.

697
00:36:30.947 --> 00:36:33.998
Here is the Java code, StoryBankL1.java.

698
00:36:34.278 --> 00:36:37.990
It runs to 72 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

699
00:36:38.270 --> 00:36:47.422
L1, Story bank coverage: which competencies interviewers ask about are covered by your stories, at which level. and which stories you lean on too much.

700
00:36:47.702 --> 00:36:58.326
Reads a markdown story bank ( title, - key: value lines). competencies commonly probed in behavioural or leadership rounds, with the minimum level where it's expected.

701
00:37:01.176 --> 00:37:02.093
On screen:

702
00:37:02.373 --> 00:37:26.907
8 stories; target level B3. competency from stories (level) status. ownership B1 S1(B3) S3(B2) ok. failure B1 S3(B2) thin, one story, below level. conflict B2 S2(B3) thin, one story. communication B1 S1(B3) S4(B2) S6(B3) ok. customer-focus B1 S8(B2) thin, one story,

703
00:37:27.187 --> 00:37:48.989
below level. pressure B2 S1(B3) thin, one story. technical-decision B2 S2(B3) S5(B4) S8(B2) ok. ambiguity B2 S5(B4) S8(B2) ok. mentoring B2 S4(B2) thin, one story, below level. influence B3 S2(B3) S6(B3) ok.

704
00:37:49.269 --> 00:37:50.784
The rest is in the chapter.

705
00:37:53.634 --> 00:37:54.487
Reading it:

706
00:37:54.767 --> 00:37:56.538
One gap: prioritisation.

707
00:37:56.818 --> 00:38:02.450
Staff interviewers ask what did you say no to? often, and version 1 of the bank has no answer.

708
00:38:02.730 --> 00:38:04.778
S9 in version 2 fills it.

709
00:38:05.058 --> 00:38:07.618
Ten competencies are thin or below level.

710
00:38:07.898 --> 00:38:15.493
Only story, and below level: failure, customer focus, mentoring and learning each rest on a single B2 story.

711
00:38:15.773 --> 00:38:20.231
Two of those stories (S3, S8) are also the weakest in L2.

712
00:38:20.511 --> 00:38:27.807
Only story, at level: conflict, pressure, incident, delivery, cross-team and hiring each have exactly one story.

713
00:38:29.157 --> 00:38:33.701
Three stories carry four competencies each (S1, S5, S6).

714
00:38:33.981 --> 00:38:37.416
If one of them tells badly, several competencies go with it.

715
00:38:37.696 --> 00:38:42.624
Run for B4, the tool adds org-change as a second gap and cost as thin.

716
00:38:43.974 --> 00:38:45.446
L2, Lint the stories.

717
00:38:45.726 --> 00:38:46.665
Requirement:

718
00:38:46.945 --> 00:38:50.059
Catch the mechanical problems before an interviewer does.

719
00:38:51.409 --> 00:38:54.524
Here is the Java code, StoryLintL2.java.

720
00:38:54.804 --> 00:38:58.537
It runs to 55 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

721
00:38:58.817 --> 00:39:04.193
L2, Story lint: the mechanical problems interviewers notice in behavioural answers.

722
00:39:04.473 --> 00:39:12.260
Checks: spoken length, how much is set-up versus action, we versus I in the action, numbers in the result, a specific learning.

723
00:39:15.110 --> 00:39:20.785
