727
00:39:55.121 --> 00:40:04.571
S4 Mentoring a new engineer into on-call 111 48s 24% 3:1 4 10w too short (1 min).

728
00:40:04.851 --> 00:40:13.747
S5 Choosing not to build a feature store 149 64s 24% 2:0 1 18w ok.

729
00:40:14.027 --> 00:40:24.203
S6 Aligning four teams on an API deprecatio 99 42s 31% 1:0 2 12w too short (1 min).

730
00:40:24.483 --> 00:40:33.529
S7 Hiring bar for the platform team 101 43s 30% 1:0 3 15w too short (1 min).

731
00:40:33.809 --> 00:40:49.915
S8 Customer-facing delay in slot booking 65 28s 45% 0:2 0 9w too short (1 min);. set-up 35%; action says 'we' more than 'I'; result has no numbers; learning too. thin.

732
00:40:50.195 --> 00:40:51.710
The rest is in the chapter.

733
00:40:54.560 --> 00:40:55.413
Reading it:

734
00:40:55.693 --> 00:40:57.784
S3 and S8 fail every check.

735
00:40:58.064 --> 00:41:02.501
Each is under 30 seconds spoken, with a set-up above 35%.

736
00:41:02.781 --> 00:41:10.867
The action says we (0:4 and 0:2), the result has no numbers, and the learning is a one-liner (Test more languages).

737
00:41:11.147 --> 00:41:15.371
These are what interviewers write down as unclear ownership, no impact.

738
00:41:15.651 --> 00:41:16.803
The rewrites pass.

739
00:41:17.083 --> 00:41:24.272
Each is 60 to 70 seconds as notes, with first-person actions, numbers in the result, and a specific learning.

740
00:41:24.552 --> 00:41:33.619
The rewrite of S3 also raises it to B3: it now shows owning the go or no-go decision and changing the process for every search change.

741
00:41:34.969 --> 00:41:38.297
S4, S6 and S7 are flagged only as short.

742
00:41:38.577 --> 00:41:39.921
They are compressed notes.

743
00:41:40.201 --> 00:41:43.998
Rehearse the spoken version to 90 to 150 seconds.

744
00:41:44.278 --> 00:41:48.395
The check is there to prompt that rehearsal, not to rewrite a good story.

745
00:41:48.675 --> 00:41:52.217
The lint is mechanical: it can't tell whether a story is good.

746
00:41:52.497 --> 00:41:54.566
That's what mocks are for (Ch 60).

747
00:41:55.916 --> 00:41:58.519
L3, Evidence behind an impact number.

748
00:41:58.799 --> 00:41:59.737
Requirement:

749
00:42:00.017 --> 00:42:02.641
My story says I made order history faster.

750
00:42:02.921 --> 00:42:06.463
Say by how much, in a way that survives 'how did you measure that?'.

751
00:42:06.743 --> 00:42:10.071
This lab runs against real PostgreSQL 16:

752
00:42:10.351 --> 00:42:11.972
1.5M orders.

753
00:42:12.252 --> 00:42:14.833
The before state: an index on customerid.

754
00:42:15.113 --> 00:42:19.231
The after state: a covering index on (customerid, createdat DESC).

755
00:42:19.511 --> 00:42:25.932
Three rounds of warm-up followed by 300 timed queries per state, with bootstrap confidence intervals.

756
00:42:27.282 --> 00:42:30.269
Here is the Java code, ImpactL3.java.

757
00:42:30.549 --> 00:42:34.730
It runs to 115 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

758
00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:37.378
L3, Evidence for an impact claim.

759
00:42:37.658 --> 00:42:42.117
Your story says I cut the order-history p99 by X%.

760
00:42:42.397 --> 00:42:59.719
Measure it honestly on. real PostgreSQL: before or after latency samples, p50 or p99 with bootstrap 95% confidence intervals, and the claims. that would be misleading (best run, mean instead of p99, a single measurement).

761
00:43:02.569 --> 00:43:03.487
On screen:

762
00:43:03.767 --> 00:43:19.703
1.5M orders, 200k customers; query: a customer's orders in the last 90 days, newest. first (the order-history page). before: index on (customerid), after: covering index on (customerid, createdat.

763
00:43:19.983 --> 00:43:28.196
DESC) INCLUDE (totalpaise, status). round state samples best ms mean ms p50 ms p99 ms.

764
00:43:28.476 --> 00:43:35.793
1 before 300 0.05 0.22 0.13 1.58.

765
00:43:36.073 --> 00:43:43.284
1 after 300 0.02 0.18 0.08 2.49.

766
00:43:43.564 --> 00:43:51.159
2 before 300 0.06 0.14 0.09 1.22.

767
00:43:51.439 --> 00:43:58.948
2 after 300 0.02 0.09 0.06 1.44.

768
00:43:59.228 --> 00:44:07.271
3 before 300 0.02 0.07 0.03 1.96.

769
00:44:07.551 --> 00:44:15.188
3 after 300 0.02 0.03 0.03 0.11.

770
00:44:15.468 --> 00:44:16.983
The rest is in the chapter.

771
00:44:19.833 --> 00:44:20.686
Reading it:

772
00:44:20.966 --> 00:44:24.273
The rounds disagree even though each round looks precise.

773
00:44:24.553 --> 00:44:34.345
The p50 reduction is 38%, 37% and 11%, and each round's 95% CI is only a few points wide.

774
00:44:34.625 --> 00:44:43.371
By round 3, the before state was also faster (p50 0.03 ms), because the table's pages were cached by then.

775
00:44:43.651 --> 00:44:48.665
A single benchmark's confidence interval does not capture this between-run variation.

776
00:44:48.945 --> 00:44:53.446
The honest claim is a range, from repeated runs and ideally from production.

777
00:44:54.796 --> 00:44:57.399
p99 has no measurable change.

778
00:44:57.679 --> 00:45:00.239
The intervals span from much worse to much better.

779
00:45:00.519 --> 00:45:02.929
Faster here means the median, not the tail.

780
00:45:03.209 --> 00:45:17.076
The same round-1 data gives three misleading claims: pairing the worst before with the best after gives 99% faster;. a single pair of queries gives 70%;. comparing means gives 19%.

781
00:45:18.426 --> 00:45:19.770
An honest story line:

782
00:45:20.050 --> 00:45:27.922
It cut the median order-history query by roughly 10 to 40% in repeated load tests at sub-millisecond latencies.

783
00:45:28.202 --> 00:45:30.015
The tail didn't measurably change.

784
00:45:30.295 --> 00:45:33.943
In production we tracked it with the p50 dashboard for a month.

785
00:45:34.223 --> 00:45:36.591
Absolute times will differ on your hardware.

786
00:45:36.871 --> 00:45:38.258
The method is the point.

787
00:45:38.538 --> 00:45:39.754
Your run will differ.

788
00:45:40.034 --> 00:45:42.573
Rerunning the lab gives different percentages.

789
00:45:42.853 --> 00:45:47.781
The pattern (per-round intervals tighter than the spread between rounds) is what to look for.

790
00:45:49.131 --> 00:45:50.517
L4, Plan the loop.

791
00:45:50.797 --> 00:45:51.736
Requirement:

792
00:45:52.016 --> 00:45:54.789
Twelve likely questions across four interviewers.

793
00:45:55.069 --> 00:46:01.256
Pick a story for each, with no story told to more than two interviewers and none repeated with the same one.

794
00:46:02.606 --> 00:46:05.571
Here is the Java code, LoopPlanL4.java.

795
00:46:05.851 --> 00:46:10.182
It runs to 154 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

796
00:46:10.462 --> 00:46:14.281
L4, Plan which story answers which question across a whole loop.

797
00:46:14.561 --> 00:46:21.345
Interviewers compare notes, so a story may be. used at most twice in the loop and never twice with the same interviewer.

798
00:46:21.625 --> 00:46:22.265
Fit:

799
00:46:22.545 --> 00:46:31.270
2 = story tagged for the competency. at or above the target level, 1 = tagged but below level, 0 = not tagged.

800
00:46:31.550 --> 00:46:34.238
Optimal plan by min-cost flow; compared.

801
00:46:37.088 --> 00:47:02.709
On screen: === story bank v1 (8 stories), staff (B3) loop, 4 interviews x 3 questions. interview competency greedy optimal. hiring manager ownership S1 S1. hiring manager conflict S2 S2. hiring manager delivery S6 S6. bar raiser failure S3~ S3~. bar raiser influence S2 S2. bar raiser customer-focus

802
00:47:02.989 --> 00:47:19.971
S8~ S8~. peer staff eng technical-decision S5 S5. peer staff eng mentoring S4~ S4~. peer staff eng cross-team S6 S6. director strategy S5 S7. director prioritisation - -.

803
00:47:20.251 --> 00:47:21.765
The rest is in the chapter.

804
00:47:24.615 --> 00:47:25.469
Reading it:

805
00:47:25.749 --> 00:47:30.463
Version 1 leaves the director's prioritisation question with no story.

806
00:47:30.743 --> 00:47:38.786
Version 2 fills it with S9, and the rewritten S3 now fits at level, so fit goes from 19 to 22 of 24.

807
00:47:39.066 --> 00:47:41.967
Greedy and optimal planning score the same on this bank.

808
00:47:42.247 --> 00:47:49.735
The optimal plan differs only by moving strategy to S7, which leaves 3 stories told twice instead of 4.

809
00:47:50.015 --> 00:47:53.215
With a bank this size, the gaps matter more than the algorithm.

810
00:47:53.495 --> 00:48:03.117
Even the optimal plan tells S1, S2 and S6 to two interviewers each, and mentoring and customer focus are still answered with B2 stories.

811
00:48:04.467 --> 00:48:14.003
The next step is not better planning but more stories: a second incident, a second influence story, and a B3 mentoring story (growing a senior engineer).

812
00:48:15.353 --> 00:48:16.291
Part 6.

813
00:48:16.571 --> 00:48:17.851
Practice Concerns.

814
00:48:19.101 --> 00:48:20.296
Mining your history.

815
00:48:20.576 --> 00:48:27.979
Look through performance reviews, promotion documents, incident postmortems, design docs, and emails where someone thanked you.

816
00:48:28.259 --> 00:48:32.952
Keep a brag document going forward: date, what you did, the numbers, and who saw it.

817
00:48:34.302 --> 00:48:35.582
Confidentiality.

818
00:48:35.862 --> 00:48:40.619
Anonymise company-specific details, and never disclose confidential numbers.

819
00:48:40.899 --> 00:48:46.019
Use relative changes (cut by about a third) if absolute numbers are sensitive.

820
00:48:46.299 --> 00:48:48.411
Don't criticise named former colleagues.

821
00:48:49.761 --> 00:48:50.785
Authenticity.

822
00:48:51.065 --> 00:48:52.644
Use real stories only.

823
00:48:52.924 --> 00:48:56.743
Probing follow-ups expose invented or inflated ones quickly.

824
00:48:57.023 --> 00:48:59.049
Rehearse the structure, not a script.

825
00:48:59.329 --> 00:49:01.740
Memorised paragraphs sound memorised.

826
00:49:03.090 --> 00:49:05.223
Cultural and communication style.

827
00:49:05.503 --> 00:49:09.471
Some engineers are trained to credit the team and play down their own role.

828
00:49:09.751 --> 00:49:15.021
In these interviews, being clear about your contribution is expected, and it isn't arrogance.

829
00:49:15.301 --> 00:49:17.967
Speak at a steady pace, and pause for follow-ups.

830
00:49:19.317 --> 00:49:20.320
Remote rounds.

831
00:49:20.600 --> 00:49:23.928
Keep a one-line cue per story visible, never full text.

832
00:49:24.208 --> 00:49:26.384
Look at the camera when making key points.

833
00:49:27.734 --> 00:49:28.673
Part 7.

834
00:49:28.953 --> 00:49:30.659
Anti-Patterns and Pitfalls.

835
00:49:31.909 --> 00:49:32.827
Watch out:

836
00:49:33.107 --> 00:49:35.688
1. \We\ all the way through.

837
00:49:35.968 --> 00:49:37.483
The interviewer can't score you.

838
00:49:38.833 --> 00:49:39.750
Watch out:

839
00:49:40.030 --> 00:49:40.734
2.

840
00:49:41.014 --> 00:49:42.166
Hypotheticals.

841
00:49:42.446 --> 00:49:44.281
I would... instead of I did....

842
00:49:45.631 --> 00:49:46.548
Watch out:

843
00:49:46.828 --> 00:49:47.575
3.

844
00:49:47.855 --> 00:49:49.412
Situation monologues.

845
00:49:49.692 --> 00:49:52.700
Three minutes of context and thirty seconds of action.

846
00:49:54.050 --> 00:49:54.967
Watch out:

847
00:49:55.247 --> 00:49:55.951
4.

848
00:49:56.231 --> 00:49:57.277
No numbers.

849
00:49:57.557 --> 00:49:59.413
The result stays unverifiable.

850
00:50:00.763 --> 00:50:01.680
Watch out:

851
00:50:01.960 --> 00:50:02.728
5.

852
00:50:03.008 --> 00:50:05.013
Inflated or cherry-picked numbers.

853
00:50:05.293 --> 00:50:07.619
These fall apart under probing (L3).

854
00:50:08.969 --> 00:50:09.886
Watch out:

855
00:50:10.166 --> 00:50:10.913
6.

856
00:50:11.193 --> 00:50:12.259
Fake failures.

857
00:50:12.539 --> 00:50:13.755
I work too hard.

858
00:50:15.105 --> 00:50:16.023
Watch out:

859
00:50:16.303 --> 00:50:17.049
7.

860
00:50:17.329 --> 00:50:18.417
Blaming others.

861
00:50:18.697 --> 00:50:21.940
It is a red flag in every failure and conflict story.

862
00:50:23.290 --> 00:50:24.207
Watch out:

863
00:50:24.487 --> 00:50:25.170
8.

864
00:50:25.450 --> 00:50:26.879
Below-level stories.

865
00:50:27.159 --> 00:50:30.466
A conflict with a junior engineer, told for a staff role.

866
00:50:31.816 --> 00:50:32.733
Watch out:

867
00:50:33.013 --> 00:50:33.760
9.

868
00:50:34.040 --> 00:50:36.024
Same story for every interviewer.

869
00:50:36.304 --> 00:50:38.693
It makes the evidence look thin (L4).

870
00:50:40.043 --> 00:50:40.961
Watch out:

871
00:50:41.241 --> 00:50:41.945
10.

872
00:50:42.225 --> 00:50:43.697
Nothing you'd do differently.

873
00:50:43.977 --> 00:50:45.811
It signals low self-awareness.

874
00:50:47.161 --> 00:50:48.079
Watch out:

875
00:50:48.359 --> 00:50:49.191
11.

876
00:50:49.471 --> 00:50:51.199
Stories older than a decade.

877
00:50:51.479 --> 00:50:54.145
These are fine for early-career examples only.

878
00:50:54.425 --> 00:50:56.004
The evidence should be recent.

879
00:50:57.354 --> 00:50:58.271
Watch out:

880
00:50:58.551 --> 00:50:59.341
12.

881
00:50:59.621 --> 00:51:01.391
Criticising a current employer.

882
00:51:01.671 --> 00:51:04.317
It raises doubts about how you'd talk about this one.

883
00:51:05.667 --> 00:51:06.541
Part 8.

884
00:51:06.821 --> 00:51:08.165
Interview Q and A.

885
00:51:09.415 --> 00:51:11.655
These questions are ones you'll be asked.

886
00:51:11.935 --> 00:51:16.330
The answers describe how to answer, with an outline from the illustrative bank.

887
00:51:17.680 --> 00:51:18.512
Beginner.

888
00:51:18.792 --> 00:51:19.816
Question 1.

889
00:51:20.096 --> 00:51:21.376
Tell me about yourself.

890
00:51:21.656 --> 00:51:23.021
Thirty-second answer:

891
00:51:23.301 --> 00:51:33.115
Ninety seconds: current role and scope, two or three highlights with numbers that match the job, what you're looking for next and why this role, a trailer, not your CV.

892
00:51:34.465 --> 00:51:35.467
Question 2.

893
00:51:35.747 --> 00:51:37.347
Tell me about a time you failed.

894
00:51:37.627 --> 00:51:38.993
Thirty-second answer:

895
00:51:39.273 --> 00:51:48.553
A real failure where you made the call, with consequences; what you did immediately; the systemic fix; and what you do differently now, owned in the first person.

896
00:51:49.903 --> 00:51:50.948
Question 3.

897
00:51:51.228 --> 00:51:52.977
Why are you leaving your current role?

898
00:51:53.257 --> 00:51:54.623
Thirty-second answer:

899
00:51:54.903 --> 00:52:02.049
Talk about what you want next, scope, problems, growth, connected to this role; stay positive about your current employer.

900
00:52:03.399 --> 00:52:04.466
Question 4.

901
00:52:04.746 --> 00:52:06.197
What's your biggest weakness?

902
00:52:06.477 --> 00:52:07.842
Thirty-second answer:

903
00:52:08.122 --> 00:52:14.373
A real, relevant weakness, a concrete example, and what you do about it with evidence it's improving.

904
00:52:15.723 --> 00:52:16.725
Intermediate.

905
00:52:17.005 --> 00:52:18.115
Question 5.

906
00:52:18.395 --> 00:52:20.699
Tell me about a disagreement with a colleague.

907
00:52:20.979 --> 00:52:22.344
Thirty-second answer:

908
00:52:22.624 --> 00:52:35.765
A real technical disagreement; how you understood their view; the evidence you brought; how it was decided; the outcome; and the relationship afterwards, at your level, ideally with someone senior or another team.

909
00:52:37.115 --> 00:52:38.225
Question 6.

910
00:52:38.505 --> 00:52:41.342
Tell me about a time you handled a production incident.

911
00:52:41.622 --> 00:52:42.987
Thirty-second answer:

912
00:52:43.267 --> 00:52:55.491
Your role, the first minutes (declare, roles, what changed), mitigation before root cause, communication cadence, recovery time and impact, and the postmortem actions you owned and followed through.

913
00:52:56.841 --> 00:52:57.929
Question 7.

914
00:52:58.209 --> 00:52:59.809
How have you helped someone grow?

915
00:53:00.089 --> 00:53:01.455
Thirty-second answer:

916
00:53:01.735 --> 00:53:08.988
A specific person and goal, your plan (pairing, stretch ownership, feedback cadence), their measurable outcome, and how it spread.

917
00:53:10.338 --> 00:53:11.362
Question 8.

918
00:53:11.642 --> 00:53:14.949
Tell me about a time you went beyond the ticket for a customer.

919
00:53:15.229 --> 00:53:16.594
Thirty-second answer:

920
00:53:16.874 --> 00:53:25.194
A customer problem you investigated at journey level, the root cause, the fix, a measured customer outcome, and a change in how you work.

921
00:53:26.544 --> 00:53:27.611
Question 9.

922
00:53:27.891 --> 00:53:29.896
How do you handle an unclear problem?

923
00:53:30.176 --> 00:53:31.541
Thirty-second answer:

924
00:53:31.821 --> 00:53:42.893
Investigate before solving, talk to users, measure current behaviour, write the problem statement, generate options with costs, take a reversible first step, and set a checkpoint.

925
00:53:44.243 --> 00:53:45.011
Senior.

926
00:53:45.291 --> 00:53:46.337
Question 10.

927
00:53:46.617 --> 00:53:50.798
Tell me about a time you influenced teams you had no authority over.

928
00:53:51.078 --> 00:53:52.443
Thirty-second answer:

929
00:53:52.723 --> 00:54:04.542
Make the cost and the deadline visible, make the path easy (libraries, office hours), agree dated milestones with each lead, escalate early with a proposed fix, and report the outcome.

930
00:54:05.892 --> 00:54:07.023
Question 11.

931
00:54:07.303 --> 00:54:09.521
How do you know the impact you claim is real?

932
00:54:09.801 --> 00:54:11.167
Thirty-second answer:

933
00:54:11.447 --> 00:54:23.265
Define the metric and percentile up front, compare like with like over enough time, repeat measurements, report ranges, separate your change from others, and prefer production metrics over one benchmark.

934
00:54:24.615 --> 00:54:25.703
Question 12.

935
00:54:25.983 --> 00:54:29.631
Tell me about a technical decision you're proud of, and one you regret.

936
00:54:29.911 --> 00:54:31.277
Thirty-second answer:

937
00:54:31.557 --> 00:54:36.485
Proud: a decision with clear options, reasoning, and a measured outcome (S5).

938
00:54:36.765 --> 00:54:42.695
Regret: a decision where you missed a consideration, how you noticed, and what changed in your process.

939
00:54:44.045 --> 00:54:45.325
Question 13.

940
00:54:45.605 --> 00:54:50.021
How do you run design reviews that improve decisions rather than slow them?

941
00:54:50.301 --> 00:54:51.667
Thirty-second answer:

942
00:54:51.947 --> 00:55:07.264
Written design docs with options and trade-offs, clear review criteria, the right reviewers, async comments first and a short meeting for open questions, decisions recorded as ADRs with revisit triggers, and tracking review lead time.

943
00:55:08.614 --> 00:55:09.809
Question 14.

944
00:55:10.089 --> 00:55:11.731
How do you disagree and commit?

945
00:55:12.011 --> 00:55:13.377
Thirty-second answer:

946
00:55:13.657 --> 00:55:25.710
Argue with evidence before the decision, state what would change your mind, and once decided, execute fully and help it succeed, while recording the revisit condition so the question can be reopened with data.

947
00:55:27.060 --> 00:55:27.956
Architect.

948
00:55:28.236 --> 00:55:29.431
Question 15.

949
00:55:29.711 --> 00:55:32.996
Tell me about a time you changed how your organisation works.

950
00:55:33.276 --> 00:55:34.641
Thirty-second answer:

951
00:55:34.921 --> 00:55:44.735
A system-of-work problem you chose (with data), the mechanism you introduced, how you got adoption across teams and leadership, measured outcomes, and what you'd change.

952
00:55:46.085 --> 00:55:47.279
Question 16.

953
00:55:47.559 --> 00:55:49.586
Tell me about a big bet that didn't work.

954
00:55:49.866 --> 00:55:51.231
Thirty-second answer:

955
00:55:51.511 --> 00:56:04.802
The bet and the reasoning at the time, the signals that it wasn't working, how and when you stopped or changed course, the cost, and what you changed in how the org makes bets (for example, revisit triggers, smaller first steps).

956
00:56:06.152 --> 00:56:07.496
Question 17.

957
00:56:07.776 --> 00:56:10.677
How do you set technical direction for multiple teams?

958
00:56:10.957 --> 00:56:12.323
Thirty-second answer:

959
00:56:12.603 --> 00:56:28.176
Understand business goals and current pain with data, write a direction document with principles and a few big bets, involve teams early, define paved roads that make the direction easy, sequence reversible steps, and review progress with measurable signals.

960
00:56:29.526 --> 00:56:30.699
Question 18.

961
00:56:30.979 --> 00:56:33.646
How do you raise the engineering bar through hiring?

962
00:56:33.926 --> 00:56:35.291
Thirty-second answer:

963
00:56:35.571 --> 00:56:47.646
Anchored rubrics per interview and level, written feedback before debriefs, calibration on recorded interviews, interviewer pass-rate reviews, and tracking hire outcomes, without slowing time-to-hire.

964
00:56:48.996 --> 00:56:50.233
Question 19.

965
00:56:50.513 --> 00:56:53.948
How do you manage up when leadership wants something you think is wrong?

966
00:56:54.228 --> 00:56:55.593
Thirty-second answer:

967
00:56:55.873 --> 00:57:09.868
Understand the goal behind the ask, present options with costs and risks in their terms, recommend one, agree what would change the decision, then commit and deliver or escalate transparently if it's a safety or legal issue.

968
00:57:11.218 --> 00:57:12.327
Question 20.

969
00:57:12.607 --> 00:57:15.893
What kind of technical leader are you, and what would your peers say?

970
00:57:16.173 --> 00:57:17.538
Thirty-second answer:

971
00:57:17.818 --> 00:57:33.349
Describe your approach through mechanisms and examples (I lead with written options and data, I grow people through ownership, I make hard calls with revisit triggers), then give specific peer feedback you've received, including a developmental point.

972
00:57:34.699 --> 00:57:35.595
Part 9.

973
00:57:35.875 --> 00:57:37.283
Summary and Practice.

974
00:57:38.533 --> 00:57:39.621
Key takeaways.

975
00:57:39.901 --> 00:57:44.765
Behavioural rounds score evidence of scope, judgement and influence at your level.

976
00:57:45.045 --> 00:57:51.487
Build a story bank that covers every competency expected at your level, with at least two stories each (L1).

977
00:57:51.767 --> 00:57:55.074
STAR+L, first person, numbers, a real learning.

978
00:57:55.354 --> 00:57:56.933
Lint your stories (L2).

979
00:57:57.213 --> 00:58:02.418
Back impact claims honestly: percentiles, repeated measurements, ranges (L3).

980
00:58:02.698 --> 00:58:05.749
Plan the loop so the evidence doesn't repeat (L4).

981
00:58:07.099 --> 00:58:12.112
At staff and principal levels, show mechanisms and systems of work, not heroics.

982
00:58:13.462 --> 00:58:14.443
Cheat sheet.

983
00:58:14.723 --> 00:58:16.067
Here is a quick table:

984
00:58:16.347 --> 00:58:18.651
Question family, and Story must show.

985
00:58:18.931 --> 00:58:19.785
Conflict:

986
00:58:20.065 --> 00:58:23.947
Evidence, respect, a condition for changing your mind, the outcome.

987
00:58:24.227 --> 00:58:25.081
Failure:

988
00:58:25.361 --> 00:58:29.307
Your mistake, consequences, systemic fix, what you do differently.

989
00:58:29.587 --> 00:58:30.505
Influence:

990
00:58:30.785 --> 00:58:34.667
Visible cost, an easy path, milestones, early escalation.

991
00:58:34.947 --> 00:58:35.801
Incident:

992
00:58:36.081 --> 00:58:39.537
Roles, mitigation first, communication, owned actions.

993
00:58:39.817 --> 00:58:40.734
Mentoring:

994
00:58:41.014 --> 00:58:42.934
A plan, their outcome, it spreading.

995
00:58:43.214 --> 00:58:44.430
Prioritisation:

996
00:58:44.710 --> 00:58:47.377
Criteria, transparency, a date for later.

997
00:58:47.657 --> 00:58:48.638
Strategy:

998
00:58:48.918 --> 00:58:51.478
Data, options, reversibility, a trigger.

999
00:58:52.828 --> 00:58:53.959
Exercises.

1000
00:58:54.239 --> 00:58:58.356
(B1) Write three stories in STAR+L and run L2 on them.

1001
00:58:58.636 --> 00:59:02.327
(B2) Tag 8 stories and run L1 for your target level.

1002
00:59:02.607 --> 00:59:03.631
Fill one gap.

1003
00:59:03.911 --> 00:59:10.972
(B3) For your best impact claim, write down the metric, the percentile, the time window and the source, as in L3.

1004
00:59:11.252 --> 00:59:14.879
(B3) Run L4 with the focus areas your recruiter shared.

1005
00:59:15.159 --> 00:59:21.196
(B4) Write your changed how the org works story, and have a peer probe it with §2.11.

1006
00:59:22.546 --> 00:59:23.549
Further reading.

1007
00:59:23.829 --> 00:59:29.375
Structured and behavioural interviewing literature (VERIFY which approach your target company uses).

1008
00:59:29.655 --> 00:59:33.239
Published leadership principles and values of your target companies.

1009
00:59:33.519 --> 00:59:36.762
Books on staff-plus engineering and technical leadership.

